* definition
	* 2 functions are Legendre transform of each other when their slopes are inverses of each other
	* this definition does not fully determine one function from the other, but it does up to shifts with a constant
* suppose f and g have iverible slopes
* proposition
	* are equivalent
		* slope g = inverse (slope f)
			* "slopes connection"
		* forall x, y
			* (y = slope f x) or (x = slope g y)
				* "variable connection"
			* f x + g y = x * y
				* upto constant shift
				* "sum equation"
* proof
	* =>
		* equation for an anti-slope generally
			* forall h x. h x = integral (from 0 to x) (slope h)
		* take arbitrary x, y
			* substitute the anti-slope formula for f and g into the "sum equation"
				* integral (from 0 to y) (inverse (slope f)) + integral (from 0 to x) (slope g) = x * y
					* this equation is true
						* proof
							* geometrically
								* the sum of (the integral of function) and (the integral of its inverse) extend each other to a rectangle area
									* with size (x * y)
	* <=
		* take the equation that determines g in terms of f and the slope of it and the inverse of that
		* take the slope of this equation; do not get frightened by the multiplicity of slopes and inverses, the complex terms will cancel each other out, really
* properties
